# These findings are pip's own internally-vendored copies of msgpack and
# setuptools (pip/_vendor/vendor.txt), not packages this project installs
# or imports. Confirmed via `pip show`: the actual installed msgpack and
# setuptools are already current. pip itself is already at its latest
# release (26.2.1) and that release is what ships these vendored versions --
# there is no newer pip to bump to that vendors a fixed copy, and no way to
# upgrade pip's private bundle independently of pip itself. Nothing in this
# codebase imports pip's vendored copies; they're used only by pip's own
# internal dependency resolution at install time.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273

# Same situation, pip's vendored urllib3==2.7.0 (also pip/_vendor/vendor.txt,
# same pip 26.2.1 release, same "no way to upgrade pip's bundle separately"
# constraint as above). Confirmed via the built image: only one real urllib3
# install exists (/opt/venv/.../urllib3-2.8.0.dist-info, matching
# requirements.txt) -- Trivy's Python package scanner also picks up pip's
# internal pip/_vendor/urllib3 source tree as if it were a second top-level
# install, and reports it at the version pip itself vendors.
CVE-2026-97687
CVE-2026-97689
